From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] mmc: sunxi: run the HS400 module clock at twice the card clock

HS400 is 8-bit DDR: the card drives data on both edges of the card clock, so
the controller has to move two bytes per card clock. The driver did not double
the module clock for it -- the DDR52 branch tests for MMC_TIMING_MMC_DDR52 and
HS400 is a different timing constant, so it fell through with div = 1.

Silicon, read under load on the 8 GiB board with `mmc-sunxi-set-the-dma-mask-from-the-descriptor-shift` applied:

  SMHC2_CLK   ours 0x81000003   vendor 0x83000001
  SMHC_CLKDIV      0x00010000          0x00010000

Decoded per the A733 user manual (CLK_SRC_SEL 001 = PERI0_800M,
011 = PERI1_800M): ours is 800/4/1 = 200 MHz module against a 200 MHz card
clock, the vendor's is 800/2/1 = 400 MHz against the same 200 MHz card clock.

The cost is exactly what one edge per clock buys. mmc tracepoints over a
64 MiB direct read, 1 MiB per request, with the mmc IRQ and the reader both on
an A76:

  requests=64  bus_time avg=6129 us per MiB  dead_time avg=182 us (3 %)
  wall 0.4037 s -> 166 MB/s

97 % of the time is inside the transfer, not between requests, and 171 MB/s on
the bus is HS200 speed on a bus that is meant to be twice that. The vendor
reaches 325 MB/s on the same card at the same card clock.

The two comments this replaces contradicted each other in the same function --
one said HS400 shares the DDR52 doubling and cited a 400 MHz module clock, the
next said it does not and pointed at HS200 as the precedent. HS200 is SDR, so
it is not a precedent for a DDR mode; that comparison is where the 1x came
from.

CCLK_DIV stays 0, unchanged: the UM requires it cleared while HS400_MD_EN is
set, and that is independent of the module clock.

Not hardware-tested yet. The test is a cold boot that reaches HS400, passes
read/write/md5, and shows bus_time per MiB dropping toward 3000 us.

Assisted-by: Claude
---
--- a/drivers/mmc/host/sunxi-mmc.c
+++ b/drivers/mmc/host/sunxi-mmc.c
@@ -918,36 +918,31 @@
 		return 0;
 
 	/*
-	 * Under the old timing mode, 8 bit DDR requires the module
-	 * clock to be double the card clock. Under the new timing
-	 * mode, all DDR modes require a doubled module clock. HS400 is
-	 * 8-bit DDR and uses the same 2x module clock: silicon shows a
-	 * 200 MHz HS400 card (module 400 MHz, CCLK_DIV=0) sustains reads,
-	 * whereas a 4x module (800 MHz) is too fast and no data-strobe
-	 * delay locks. So HS400 shares the DDR52 divider here.
+	 * Under the old timing mode, 8 bit DDR requires the module clock to be
+	 * double the card clock. Under the new timing mode, all DDR modes
+	 * require a doubled module clock.
+	 *
+	 * HS400 is 8-bit DDR and needs that doubling for the same reason: the
+	 * card drives data on both clock edges, so the controller has to move
+	 * two bytes per card clock. Running the module clock at 1x lets the
+	 * bus initialise and transfer correctly, but it cannot fill it --
+	 * measured on the Cubie A7S, 6129 us per MiB, which is 171 MB/s where
+	 * the vendor stack reaches 325 MB/s on the same card at the same
+	 * 200 MHz card clock. 171 MB/s is HS200 speed: exactly what one edge
+	 * per clock buys.
+	 *
+	 * CCLK_DIV stays 0 for HS400 either way (it must be 0 while
+	 * HS400_MD_EN is set, UM SMHC_CLKDIV), which is what
+	 * clkdiv = 1 encodes here -- the register takes clkdiv - 1.
 	 */
-	if (ios->timing == MMC_TIMING_MMC_DDR52 &&
-	    (host->use_new_timings ||
-	     ios->bus_width == MMC_BUS_WIDTH_8)) {
+	if ((ios->timing == MMC_TIMING_MMC_DDR52 &&
+	     (host->use_new_timings ||
+	      ios->bus_width == MMC_BUS_WIDTH_8)) ||
+	    ios->timing == MMC_TIMING_MMC_HS400) {
 		div = 2;
 		clock <<= 1;
 	}
 
-	/*
-	 * HS400 does NOT take the DDR52 doubling. Under the new timing mode
-	 * the module clock already equals the card clock and the controller
-	 * derives the DDR edges itself -- which is exactly how HS200 runs on
-	 * this board: 200 MHz module, 200 MHz card, NTSR MODE_SEL set, and
-	 * not a single data error. The A/B that established this is
-	 * the following: one
-	 * kernel, one card, HS200 versus HS400 with only the DT property
-	 * removed, eight of eleven controller registers byte-identical, and
-	 * the whole difference in the timing mode plus the module/card ratio.
-	 *
-	 * CCLK_DIV stays 0 regardless (it must be 0 while HS400_MD_EN is set,
-	 * UM SMHC_CLKDIV), which is what clkdiv = 1 encodes
-	 * here -- the register takes clkdiv - 1.
-	 */
 	if (ios->timing == MMC_TIMING_MMC_HS400)
 		clkdiv = 1;
 
